# LaTeX Engineering

Distilled from: Overleaf "Management in a large project", thetapad.com large LaTeX projects, dmsussman.org LaTeX collaboration guide, NeurIPS / ICLR style files.

## File layout (already established)

- `main.tex` is orchestration only: preamble, title, `\input{sections/...}`, bibliography, appendix. No prose body.
- One section per file under `sections/`, prefixed by 2-digit order (`01_introduction.tex`, ...).
- Figures: TikZ source under `figures/<name>.tex`, output `figures/<name>.pdf`. See `AGENTS.md` rule 1 for style preset.
- Tables that are large or generated from scripts: separate file under `tables/`, `\input` from the relevant section.
- All packages and custom macros live in `main.tex` preamble (or, if it grows past ~30 packages, factor into `preamble.tex`). Do not `\usepackage` inside section files.
- Bibliography in `references.bib` only. No inline `thebibliography` and no second .bib file.

## When editing sections

- Prefer `\input{sections/XX_name}` over `\include{}` for sections (we want them on the same page flow).
- Use `\includeonly{sections/05_training}` in `main.tex` only as a temporary local edit when iterating fast on one section. Remove before commit.
- Cross-reference: always use `\label{type:name}` with one of these prefixes — `sec:`, `subsec:`, `fig:`, `tab:`, `eq:`, `app:`, `alg:`. Reference with `\cref` (cleveref) so the reference type word ("Section", "Figure") stays consistent.
- Math: define recurring symbols once in the preamble with `\newcommand`, do not redefine in sections. Examples: `\newcommand{\wer}{\mathrm{WER}}`, `\newcommand{\R}{\mathbb{R}}`.

## Bibliography rules

- BibTeX key format: `<lastname><year><firstcontentword>`, e.g. `gulati2020conformer`, `radford2023whisper`. Lowercase, no punctuation. Existing keys must not be renamed (cross-section refs would break silently).
- Every new cite must include: author (full names with `and`), year, title, booktitle / journal, volume / number / pages where applicable. Prefer the published-venue entry over the arXiv preprint when both exist; if both, keep one (published) and omit the other.
- Use `\citep{}` inside parentheses-friendly prose ("recent work \citep{radford2023whisper}"). Use `\citet{}` when the author is the grammatical subject ("\citet{gulati2020conformer} introduced …").
- Multiple cites: `\citep{a, b, c}` not `\citep{a}\citep{b}`.
- If a cite is added, run a build right away to make sure `bibtex` resolves it.

## Numbers, units, and typography

- Thousand separator inside math: `10{,}000` (the `{}` keeps the comma upright).
- Numbers and units always glued with a thin space: `960\,h`, `16\,kHz`, `7\,B`, `5e-7` is fine in tables.
- Percent sign: `5\%` (escape the `%`).
- Em-dash for parenthetical breaks: `---` (LaTeX renders as one character). Do not use a hyphen.
- Quotes: use `` ` `` and `'` for single, ``\`\` `` and `''` for double. Never `"..."`.
- Algorithm-style code or prompts: use the `lstlisting` environment with the `prompt` style already defined in `main.tex` preamble. Do not `\verb|...|` for multi-line code.

## Compile workflow

- Default: `PATH=/Library/TeX/texbin:$PATH latexmk -pdf -interaction=nonstopmode main.tex`
- After every substantive content edit, run latexmk and confirm exit code 0 before reporting completion. (Vibe Paper rule.)
- If a fresh package is needed: `sudo tlmgr install <pkg>`. Add a one-line note in `README.md` "Required tlmgr packages" so a fresh checkout can reproduce.
- Do not commit `main.pdf` (`.gitignore` already enforces). Do commit `figures/*.pdf` for reproducibility.

## What never to do

- Do not silently change the document class, geometry, or font size; these affect reviewer page count.
- Do not introduce a package whose macros conflict with `arxiv.sty`'s redefinitions of `\section`, `\maketitle`, `\abstract`. If unsure, build first and inspect the log.
- Do not use Unicode characters in source where a LaTeX command exists (use `\alpha` not `α`, `\textendash` not `–`).
